          "episode_revision_id": "b2edcd7f-c1ad-4a1b-9df6-45def6de524f",
          "title": "Finding hidden growth opportunities in your product | Albert Cheng (Duolingo, Grammarly, Chess.com)",
          "guest": "Albert Cheng",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2BKmNmnEj9w",
          "archive_member": "episodes/albert-cheng/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": " and I think the charitable understanding of this is that a lot of the small decisions that you make each day, how do you treat people? How do you show up? What's your character, et cetera. They can compound and they open doors for you in many surprising and amazing ways.\n\n(01:22:41):\nA lot of these companies that have actually joined have come through relatively light connections. And even just being on this podcast, I think I've seen a number of folks that I've worked with before beyond the show. And so I think doing the right thing, building a good reputation, they can carry you a long way. And the flip side of that is reputations are fragile too, right? So if you do the wrong thing, take a long time to repair that. So I don't know, it just stuck with me my entire life. I thought that was an interesting life motto.\n\nLenny Rachitsky (01:23:13):\nLast question. You work at Chess.com, how's your chess?\n\nAlbert Cheng (01:23:16):\nTerrible compared to serious, serious players, but quite compared to the casual ones, yeah. My yellow rating is about 1,800 for a rabbit games.\n\nLenny Rachitsky (01:23:25):\nIt sounds really-\n\nAlbert Cheng (01:23:27):\nAnd about 1,500 for blitz. Yeah, but I play many times every day.\n\nLenny Rachitsky (01:23:31):\nBlitz is like fast chess?\n\nAlbert Cheng (01:23:32):\nBlitz is like faster chess, three minute games. Rapid is more like a 10-minute game, which is still pretty fast.\n\nLenny Rachitsky (01:23:38):\nAnd you say you play multiple times a day? Do they make time? Is this like-\n\nAlbert Cheng (01:23:42):\nThey do.\n\n",
          "start_char": 93034,
          "end_char": 94593,
          "start_seconds": 4934,
          "end_seconds": 5022,
          "similarity": 0.5886472730806883
        },
        "S17": {
          "chunk_id": "0cb3575b-18e6-4c22-b066-6e56cae96428",
          "episode_id": "2139f8d7-6b1e-4ab0-88f3-33c9e1aab56d",
          "episode_revision_id": "b2edcd7f-c1ad-4a1b-9df6-45def6de524f",
          "title": "Finding hidden growth opportunities in your product | Albert Cheng (Duolingo, Grammarly, Chess.com)",
          "guest": "Albert Cheng",
          "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2BKmNmnEj9w",
          "archive_member": "episodes/albert-cheng/transcript.md",
          "archive_sha256": "5915e40e36e0511a3befc5aa22c3ed37ba4f4e0d9971367332810da71a817bf6",
          "text": "'s just like go from an idea to a representative solution. Today, there's a lot of humans involved in taking an idea, writing up a spec, doing a review, doing design, et cetera. I'm sure you've interviewed plenty of people that have talked about this specific problem.\n\n(00:18:52):\nAnd so for us, we've invested a bit in at least carving out the main screens of our product experience, things like our onboarding flow, our home screen, our chessboard as an example, and building essentially AI prototypes of those using tools like a V0 or a Lovable. And when you have those foundational pieces, you can then share them with the rest of the company and they can use that as a starting point and then they can try to put their ideas on top of that and then they become a lot more discussable and hopefully testable relatively soon.\n\nLenny Rachitsky (00:19:25):\nWhat's in your AI stack along those lines?\n\nAlbert Cheng (00:19:27):\nThe PMs are mostly using V0. The designers love Figmas, they're using Figma Make. The engineers are using a combination of tools right now. So Cursor, Cloud Code, GitHub, Copilot. Marketing teams use all sorts of tools for translation, subtitles, content adaptations, et cetera. Customers support uses Intercom then. So there's quite a lot of tools that are used across the company.\n\n",
          "start_char": 21007,
          "end_char": 22319,
          "start_seconds": 1112,
          "end_seconds": 1167,
          "similarity": 0.5878058159143972
        }
      },
      "used_context_tokens": 5902,
      "context_hit": true,
      "answer_seconds": 5.439377999864519,
      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Cheng’s advice is to start with user value and choose the AI technology that fits the job: Chess.com uses chess engines for move analysis and LLMs to make that analysis approachable and personable.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S5",
          "S8"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Match the technology to the feature",
            "content": "Keep the customer as the North Star; don’t add LLMs just because they are fashionable. For Chess.com’s game review, chess engines evaluate every move, while LLMs help present those evaluations in plain, approachable language and with a conversational personality, including audio. The aim is to make powerful chess technology useful even to people who have never moved a piece.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S5"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Use specialized engines for chess analysis",
            "content": "Cheng distinguishes chess engines from LLMs: LLMs can hallucinate moves and are not as strong at searching deeply through chess positions. For chess-specific evaluation, a trained engine is the better fit; use the LLM for how the result is communicated, rather than relying on it to produce the chess analysis.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S8",
              "S5"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
